      "question_id": "mathematics_undergraduate_doc_03_mathematics_long_1",
      "question_type": "LONG",
      "stem_text": "Find the integer closest in value to \\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{5^4+1}-\\sqrt[4]{5^4-1}}.\\]",
      "options": null,
      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "Let $a = 5^4 + 1$ and $b = 5^4-1,$ so the given expression becomes $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{a} -\n\\sqrt[4]{b}}.$ We can rationalize by multiplying top and bottom by $\\sqrt[4]{a} +\n\\sqrt[4]{b},$ and then by $\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b}$: \\[\\begin{aligned} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{a} -\n\\sqrt[4]{b}} &= \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{a}+\\sqrt[4]{b}}{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}} =\n\\frac{\\left(\\sqrt[4]{a}+\\sqrt[4]{b}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b\\right)}{a - b}.\n\\end{aligned}\\]We have $a-b=2,$ and both $a$ and $b$ are close to $5^4,$ so we estimate\nthat \\[\\frac{\\left(\\sqrt[4]{a}+\\sqrt[4]{b}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b\\right)}{a - b}\n\\approx \\frac{(5+5)(5^2+5^2)}{2} = \\boxed{250}. \\](Indeed, the actual value of the\nexpression is $249.9998599\\dots.$)",
      "gold_confidence": 1.0,
      "answer_explanation": "Students should show all steps in their mathematical solution.",
      "source": {
        "dataset": "gsm8k_math",
        "source_id": "9460"
      },
      "has_image": false,
      "image_path": null
    },
